WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.793
- [Collin Sexton:] I didn't call for the
screen just because I

00:00:01.793 --> 00:00:03.420
knew I could get around him.

00:00:03.420 --> 00:00:05.547
And I knew I had a
little bit of burst.

00:00:12.470 --> 00:00:13.513
This is Collin Sexton

00:00:13.513 --> 00:00:14.556
from the
Cleveland Cavaliers,

00:00:14.556 --> 00:00:15.015
and I'm gonna take you

00:00:15.015 --> 00:00:17.058
through some of my
most exciting plays.

00:00:17.058 --> 00:00:18.768
I'm always watching
the second defender,

00:00:18.768 --> 00:00:20.979
and I'm just pretty
much reading the big

00:00:20.979 --> 00:00:23.148
and reading if anyone's
gonna step up.

00:00:23.148 --> 00:00:24.983
I didn't call for the
screen just because I

00:00:24.983 --> 00:00:26.735
knew I could get around him.

00:00:26.735 --> 00:00:28.695
And I knew I had a
little bit of burst

00:00:28.695 --> 00:00:30.238
that I could use against him.

00:00:30.238 --> 00:00:31.865
I just told him
just to stay back,

00:00:31.865 --> 00:00:34.492
and I knew I could get
my defender on my hip.

00:00:34.492 --> 00:00:36.578
And once I did, I was able
to get in front of him.

00:00:36.578 --> 00:00:38.455
I stopped a little bit,
and I got the bump,

00:00:38.455 --> 00:00:41.124
and then I was able to shoot the
little mini-floater right here.

00:00:41.124 --> 00:00:43.376
Just my off-the-dribble
move package,

00:00:43.376 --> 00:00:46.379
just depending on the
player. The bigger guys I

00:00:46.379 --> 00:00:48.256
have to make sure I get shifted.

00:00:48.256 --> 00:00:50.633
Being one of the top players
in three-point percentage

00:00:50.633 --> 00:00:53.094
when it's clutch time just
shows that all the work that I'm

00:00:53.094 --> 00:00:55.013
putting in is gonna
pay off, and just

00:00:55.013 --> 00:00:57.766
showing the time that I spend
in the gym, and the time

00:00:57.766 --> 00:00:59.517
that I spend out
there on the floor.

00:00:59.517 --> 00:01:01.895
This was one of my best
games in my career.

00:01:01.895 --> 00:01:03.897
We didn't have that much
time left on the clock,

00:01:03.897 --> 00:01:06.024
and I knew where I
wanted to get to,

00:01:06.024 --> 00:01:09.069
and I knew Larry [Nance Jr.]
was coming up to slip out.

00:01:09.069 --> 00:01:11.488
Once he slipped out, either
if his man jumped out,

00:01:11.488 --> 00:01:12.739
Larry would've been wide open.

00:01:12.739 --> 00:01:15.075
Going left is what I
like to do, especially

00:01:15.075 --> 00:01:16.201
like pulling up from three.

00:01:16.201 --> 00:01:18.995
So I knew that once I got
a little bit of daylight,

00:01:18.995 --> 00:01:20.663
I was going to just
shoot it regardless.

00:01:20.663 --> 00:01:23.291
And miss or make, I was gonna
live with the attempt.

00:01:23.291 --> 00:01:25.126
It just came in, and
I knocked it down.

00:01:25.126 --> 00:01:27.629
I knew I had to make sure
I flipped my wrist just enough

00:01:27.629 --> 00:01:30.423
because I knew Kyrie [Irving]
was playing really good defense

00:01:30.423 --> 00:01:31.341
right here.

00:01:31.341 --> 00:01:34.344
Whenever I draw two, or
whenever I create a trap—

00:01:34.344 --> 00:01:37.680
whether it's off a pick-and-roll,
or even like a pindown

00:01:37.680 --> 00:01:39.891
where two come to
me—then I always

00:01:39.891 --> 00:01:41.976
try to look for the
nearest man or open man.

00:01:41.976 --> 00:01:43.645
And they might swing
it to someone else.

00:01:43.645 --> 00:01:47.232
I didn't realize that I get
doubled eighth in the league

00:01:47.232 --> 00:01:48.024
until now.

00:01:48.024 --> 00:01:50.151
It just shows teams,
they respect me,

00:01:50.151 --> 00:01:52.570
that I am growing, as well as
just getting better

00:01:52.570 --> 00:01:53.113
as a player.

00:01:56.699 --> 00:01:59.035
So right here, I knew
I didn't have the shot.

00:01:59.035 --> 00:02:02.163
I seen three, I think three
or four people in front of me.

00:02:02.163 --> 00:02:04.707
So I just pretty much
kicked the ball back out.

00:02:04.707 --> 00:02:06.876
Once I kicked the
ball back out, I've

00:02:06.876 --> 00:02:09.504
seen the shot clock was down,
so the only thing that I was thinking

00:02:09.504 --> 00:02:12.257
is if he swings it back, I gotta
get it up. I gotta shoot it.

00:02:12.257 --> 00:02:14.259
So once he swung
it back to me,

00:02:14.259 --> 00:02:16.803
I was ready to shoot and
in position to shoot.

00:02:16.803 --> 00:02:19.973
I'm glad I knocked it down,
because this was a big shot.

00:02:19.973 --> 00:02:23.184
Those are the types of metrics
that we pretty much think

00:02:23.184 --> 00:02:24.936
about, is trying to
go quick, or even

00:02:24.936 --> 00:02:27.605
like the two-for-one
situation side,

00:02:27.605 --> 00:02:30.024
so I can be able to get to my
spot and be able to pull up.

00:02:30.024 --> 00:02:32.443
But some of the guys that are
my height and some people that

00:02:32.443 --> 00:02:33.611
are guarding me,
I'm able to just,

00:02:33.611 --> 00:02:34.863
to pretty much get to my spot.

00:02:34.863 --> 00:02:37.532
Because I know I can get enough
elevation and enough

00:02:37.532 --> 00:02:40.618
rise in my shot that I can
pretty much shoot over him.

00:02:40.618 --> 00:02:42.662
The metrics just
pretty much just show

00:02:42.662 --> 00:02:44.664
I think I run a lot
faster than most people.

00:02:44.664 --> 00:02:47.667
That's something that I use
for my advantage on offense,

00:02:47.667 --> 00:02:49.085
as well as on defense as well.

00:02:49.085 --> 00:02:51.129
It's kind of hard to stop
the ball when someone's

00:02:51.129 --> 00:02:52.255
running full speed at you.

00:02:52.255 --> 00:02:53.631
We've been doing
pretty much just

00:02:53.631 --> 00:02:56.676
tracking my top speeds and
some of my average speeds

00:02:56.676 --> 00:02:57.427
just recently.

00:02:57.427 --> 00:02:59.554
And they've been
getting up in numbers.

00:02:59.554 --> 00:03:04.058
I can use my speed to get up
to 15, 16, 17 miles an hour.

00:03:04.058 --> 00:03:05.643
Then those are the
chances that I want

00:03:05.643 --> 00:03:07.187
to make sure I capitalize on.

00:03:07.187 --> 00:03:09.397
You're taught at a young
age: If you give it up,

00:03:09.397 --> 00:03:10.064
you get it back.

00:03:10.064 --> 00:03:11.774
That's just simple
basketball, especially when

00:03:11.774 --> 00:03:12.483
it's a two-on-one.

00:03:12.483 --> 00:03:13.985
You always give it
up to get it back.

00:03:13.985 --> 00:03:16.070
It's hard to defend that,
especially on two-on-one.

00:03:16.070 --> 00:03:17.906
If I continue to hold
the ball, I probably

00:03:17.906 --> 00:03:19.657
wouldn't even got an
easy layup like this.

00:03:19.657 --> 00:03:21.326
Just taking that
extra two more steps

00:03:21.326 --> 00:03:23.620
allow me to even get
a wide-open layup.

00:03:23.620 --> 00:03:25.371
That's just plain and
simple right there.

00:03:25.371 --> 00:03:28.208
I definitely overshot the
layup, just because I was

00:03:28.208 --> 00:03:29.459
running a little bit too fast.

00:03:29.459 --> 00:03:31.628
I'm gonna take these
metrics and just pretty much

00:03:31.628 --> 00:03:35.465
just continue watching, focus
on the areas that I do well at

00:03:35.465 --> 00:03:38.384
and just continue to get
better at those areas,

00:03:38.384 --> 00:03:41.512
as well as add more metrics
into it and more things

00:03:41.512 --> 00:03:44.265
that I feel like will
help me along the way.

00:03:44.265 --> 00:03:47.060
Thanks for checking out this
episode of "The Breakdown."

